
Ratified as amended. One definitional tightening before it goes in, because rung 1 as phrased can swallow rung 2.

## What counts as explicit

"Explicit user instruction" needs a test, or every brief becomes an override by implication — a brief that says *make it punchy* licenses violating any hedge or concision entry, and the firewall becomes decorative. The earlier override rule survives intact only if the trigger is narrow.

````
<override>
An override requires a directive that names the constrained behaviour, not a preference that
implies it. Test: could the instruction be honoured without violating the entry? If yes, it is
not an override — honour the instruction and the entry both.

  "Open with the number, no framing sentence."     → override, names the behaviour
  "Make it punchy."                                → not an override, register gesture
  "Don't hedge anywhere."                          → override, scoped global
  "Keep it confident."                             → not an override

Never inferred. An override is granted or it is not. Ambiguity resolves to not-granted, and the
gap goes to QUESTIONS.

Scope: minimum that satisfies the instruction. A targeted override suspends one entry at named
locations; it does not suspend the entry artifact-wide, and it does not suspend neighbouring
entries. Global scope only when stated globally.

Logs to T5, one line per suspended entry: entry ID, scope, instruction quoted. Suspension is
never silent — the whole point of ranking instruction above the firewall is that the cost stays
visible.

Propagation: an override enters the spec layer and continuation inherits it. Segment 2 does not
re-trip an entry segment 1 was told to suspend. Withdrawal is a correction under R14 and
invalidates built segments downstream of the change.
</override>
````

Add to `<projection>`: `T5 trailer: overrides applied`.

## Rung 3 is asymmetric

Worth stating in the ladder itself, since it will otherwise get litigated during assembly. Rungs 2, 4 and 5 are enforcement — judgments Mimic makes about a producible artifact, and an instruction can outrank a judgment. Rung 3 is capability. If the adapter cannot render a thing, an instruction to render it is not an override, because there is nothing to suspend.

> Overrides reach rungs 2, 4, 5. Rung 3 is not overridable: an instruction conflicting with an adapter hard constraint is a bounds trip, surfaced in T2, not a suspension. Raising a scale ceiling is a parameter change, not an override, and does not log to T5.

## Ladder, final

```
1  explicit user instruction        (per <override>)
2  firewall entries                 overridable
3  adapter hard constraints         not overridable — conflict surfaces as bounds trip
4  scale ceiling                    adjustable as parameter
5  mechanics and budgets            overridable
6  proposals                        yield to all above
```

## One open item, flagged rather than decided

Rung 2 is now uniformly overridable. If any firewall entry exists that should hold against direct instruction — the kind where compliance would make the artifact misrepresent something rather than merely read worse — it needs declaring as a non-overridable subset now, because assembly will hardcode the rung-2 branch. My default if you don't want to reopen it: uniform overridability, every suspension logged to T5, and the log is the safeguard. That's coherent and I'd ship it. Just naming the fork so it's a decision rather than an artifact of the ladder's shape.

## Architecture closed

Everything settled routes into assembly with no remaining conceptual gaps. Sequence from here:

1. **Assemble** in the declared load order, `<override>` attaching at rung 1 of the precedence block, `<projection>` last so it resolves T5.
2. **Single dispatch** at Step 4 on three flags — track, delivery mode, speed. Verify no branch logic survives elsewhere; the fastest way to reintroduce coupling is a second branch inside an adapter.
3. **Calibration run** with all `#asserted` entries in log-only mode. Three to four real tasks per adapter. Count natural rates, then set thresholds above what a qualitatively good artifact produces. P5 first — tightest ratio, so most data per run.
4. **Promote** entries to `#calibrated` individually as each clears the ≥3-artifact bar. No bulk promotion; an entry that hasn't been measured stays asserted however confident the number looks.

**Cost:** `<override>` ~150 tokens, ladder block ~60, T5 slot ~5. Fires only when an override is granted, so typical-path cost is the ladder alone.

Ready to assemble on your confirmation of the rung-2 fork, or on your instruction to take the uniform default.
